er  space-time values  these proper space-time distances and  angles are of course the ones that would  be measured by observers local to the  system there  now it's important to note that nowhere  are the axes of space and time actually  being skewed or stretched with respect  to one another  local observers on the manifold will  always construct the four dimensions of  space and time in a normalized  orthogonal fashion  it's just that a non-local distant  observer  may end up squashing and distorting this  construction in order to fit it to their  projection  locally time and space always look the  same  but non-locally time and space are only  coordinative projections  mere gridlines drawn on a map  so at last we have a fully geometric  interpretation of the space-time metric  and indeed it's this geometric resizing  of infinitesimal pieces that allows us  finally to resolve the problem of  determining the space-time distance on a  space-time map  given any two world events on a map the  coordinate path between them and the  metric for that map the space-time  distance is easily calculated using the  metric  plus some calculus  but that's only the beginning of the  metrics real power  because analyzing how the metric changes  from point to point on our map  can tell us about the fundamental nature  of our surface  but that's for future videos  this has been dialect thanks for  watching  you

